WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.720
Every couple of years,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.960
mega-sporting events, like the
World Cup and the Olympics,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:07.860
give us a glimpse

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.563
into the outer bounds
of human performance.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.010
Surrounding that spectacle quite literally

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.350
is an entirely different calculus,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.200
the decision to even put on
the games in the first place,

00:00:20.488 --> 00:00:21.330
Once a highly sought role,

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.190
hosting a mega-event is now

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.020
an entirely different proposition.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.090
One that politicians, governments,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.527
executives, and citizens are asking,

00:00:29.527 --> 00:00:30.537
"Is it worth it?"

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.900
What is the change in tourism

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.610
during the games and after the games,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.878
controlling for other variables?

00:00:37.878 --> 00:00:40.350
What is the change in fiscal revenues?

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.970
What is the change in employment?

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.520
What is the change in GDP, and so on?

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.830
We don't see these gains materializing.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.810
There's some big numbers that go around

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.570
for Qatar's spending on this tournament.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.120
I'm worried that the
government will be surprised

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.630
at how many people leave
after the event is all over.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.390
I can see why hosting the Olympics,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.670
and bidding on the Olympics is attractive,

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.980
but it absolutely comes with enormous

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.209
long term risks and costs.

00:01:07.209 --> 00:01:10.260
After years of controversy and criticism,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:11.220
mega sporting events

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.460
could be on the cusp of a
new, more sustainable era.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.130
When it comes to organizing the games,

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.390
there must be a debate.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.860
And we've done a lot of
reforms over the last decades,

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.440
to make sure that the
games remain relevant.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.670
But will the changes be enough

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.920
to convince potential hosts?

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.620
One city's example might
offer a way forward.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.550
We have the ability

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.740
to really fit the games to our community,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.249
as opposed to have to fit
the community to the games.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.193
The first winter World Cup,

00:01:49.193 --> 00:01:52.500
held in the compact
affluent state of Qatar,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.470
has drawn a massive number
of people to the country.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.790
Organizers are expecting

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.930
some 1.2 million people
will come to the country

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.940
over the course of this month long event.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.990
So you're essentially thinking
roughly a 10% increase

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.630
in population every night

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.480
in the most busy parts of the tournament.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:13.830
Qatar's also really interested

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.380
in developing a tourism economy.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.410
They've seen the success of Dubai.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.080
And even though this is a much
more conservative country,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.660
they think that they can draw people here

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.290
for other major sporting
events, for conferences,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.920
just to visit on
stopovers to other places.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.440
So they're hoping that
some of the new hotels,

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.660
and new entertainment venues and the like,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.340
that they've built for the World Cup,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.329
can get used by the next
generation of tourists.

00:02:41.329 --> 00:02:44.520
Authorities estimate that
hosting the World Cup

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.883
could add as much as $17
billion into its economy.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.700
But in order to get to that point,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.950
the country has spent a
massive amount of money

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.230
over the past decade on infrastructure,

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:58.560
including building an entirely new city.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.650
There are various estimates
of how much exactly Qatar

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.650
has spent in order to
prepare for this event.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.053
The stadiums are really
just a small fraction.

00:03:08.334 --> 00:03:11.970
While costs for the 2022 World Cup

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.750
are reportedly the highest ever
for a sporting competition,

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.490
it's part of an unsettling trend

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.890
for what are known as mega-events.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.270
Mega means big.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.310
Other than the day to day leagues,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:28.310
the NBA, the NFL, MLB, NHL,
or the English Premier League,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.430
the day to day leagues,

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.170
where they have every couple of days,

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.120
or if it's baseball, every
single day they have a new game,

00:03:35.983 --> 00:03:37.110
that's standard economic issues.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.060
But if it's something
where a lot of people

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.560
are coming from out of town,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.650
and there's one big event or
there's one series of events,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.330
like the Olympics is 17 days,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.270
or the World Cup has
traditionally been around 28 days,

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.430
you're doing something special
that doesn't normally happen,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.550
and involves tens of thousands,
if not millions of people,

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.203
then we call that a mega-event.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.540
The first international

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.690
modern Olympics in 1896, held in Athens,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.690
was considered the first
sporting mega-event,

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.530
the largest competition
ever held up to that time.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.530
Although initially limited
to male amateur competitors

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.630
from only 14 countries,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.920
the Olympics have expanded

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.553
significantly in size,
scope, and inclusivity since.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.750
One key tradition that has
persisted for the past century,

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.810
is that the games travel every four years.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:25.710
Every two years,

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:30.317
we have the Olympic Games
and the Winter Olympic Games,

00:04:30.317 --> 00:04:32.820
which represent what the
movement collectively does,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:36.590
and bring the best of the
sporting world together.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.120
At the helm of the modern games

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.230
is an organization called

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.300
the International Olympic Committee,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.330
made up of delegates from
participating nations.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.120
The IOC is responsible
for choosing host cities,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.980
and ensuring that the games are run

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.170
in accordance with IOC rules.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.120
The movement has evolved tremendously

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.130
over more than a century.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.530
It has gone through many developments,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.110
as sport in the end reflects society,

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.533
and to remain relevant, this
is a constant adaptation.

00:05:05.377 --> 00:05:08.280
For the first half century or so,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.713
the games were a relatively
low budget affair.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.290
But beginning in the 1960s,

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:16.710
the IOC and local planning
committees began to see value

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.520
in lucrative sponsorships
and global television rights.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.350
As the potential rewards grew,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.270
so did the scale of the events,

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.620
along with the cost to build

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.930
the infrastructure to host them.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.930
Those costs however, are
not fronted by the IOC.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.490
It's up to the host cities,

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.910
who bid for a chance to
reap the potential rewards.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.770
But is it worth it?

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.130
Somewhere between four and $6
billion of revenue will get

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.460
generated by hosting the
Summer Games in these days.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.460
On the expense side, it's
much, much, much higher.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.412
That's not a good financial balance.

00:05:49.412 --> 00:05:50.730
Projected costs

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:54.330
for an event on an Olympic
scale are expectedly high.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.280
But the real trouble
cities can find themselves

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.510
facing is when projected costs
go substantially over budget,

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.303
often forcing governments
to absorb the overruns.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.450
The University of Oxford in England

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.960
has done a number of studies
on costs and cost overruns.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.780
And what they have found
is that since 1976,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.430
the average cost overrun

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:18.470
or the medium cost overrun
for the Summer Games is 252%.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.630
So that means 3.52 times the initial bid.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.560
The whole point about these
studies is to constantly

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.610
be aware that organizing an
event like this is not a given.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.670
It's a constant strive to make sure that,

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:37.079
that the games when it comes
to its economics do balance.

00:06:37.079 --> 00:06:38.670
What drives costs

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.310
so exceedingly over
budget for mega-events?

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:44.010
Well, it's partly due to optimism bias,

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.540
in which planners simply assume

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.480
things will cost less
than they ultimately do.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.160
Promoters might even intentionally

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.150
distort initial projections
to help win over stakeholders.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.580
And by the time the
mega-event rolls around,

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.060
there's simply no other
option but to spend more.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.620
If the Olympic Games are gonna start

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.530
on July 20th of a particular year,

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:05.827
you can't call up and say,

00:07:05.827 --> 00:07:07.860
"Oh gee, we need another two weeks.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.350
The Olympic Stadium won't
be ready on July 20th."

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.030
No, that can't happen.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.640
And so once you have
that kind of a very firm

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:18.450
hard deadline in place, now
the construction companies

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.520
know that this has to be top priority.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.140
And they have to start shifting workers

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:23.460
from other construction projects,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.500
and shifting materials over there.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.790
And that gives them an excuse

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.680
to bid up the prices still more.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.260
So you have all of these
factors working together.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.804
And all of a sudden you're
two, or three, or four,

00:07:34.804 --> 00:07:36.360
or five times higher than the initial bid.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.044
Not surprisingly,

00:07:38.044 --> 00:07:40.890
enthusiasm for hosting the
games has varied over the years.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.440
For a period, the game seemed cursed.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.740
From tragic violence around
the 1968 in Mexico City

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.290
and 1972 Munich Summer Games,

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.800
to a debt-ridden financial
disaster in 1976,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.310
that Montreal spent the
next 30 years paying off.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.710
More recently, enormous budget overruns,

00:07:58.710 --> 00:07:59.850
political controversies,

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.220
and viral scenes of decaying stadiums

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.950
in Athens, Rio de Janeiro, and Beijing,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.870
have led some citizen activists

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.270
to openly question the
environmental impacts,

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.723
political optics, and cost
benefit ratio of hosting.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.110
Boston is an absolutely beautiful city.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.510
It looks great in the summertime.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:20.740
It looks great when you envision

00:08:21.674 --> 00:08:23.804
that helicopter or drone shot

00:08:23.804 --> 00:08:26.543
looking over a brand new stadium
with fireworks going off,

00:08:26.543 --> 00:08:28.050
and our gleaming skyline
in the background,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.940
and the Charles River on one hand,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.653
and Boston Harbor on the other.

00:08:33.024 --> 00:08:34.830
It's really an impressive
and gorgeous place.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.050
It's also on,

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.080
or in the most valuable
time zone in the world.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.230
The East Coast time zone has something

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:47.910
like 40 or 50% of all of
American television viewers.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:49.604
Boston beats out

00:08:49.604 --> 00:08:52.170
Los Angeles, San Francisco, and DC,

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:53.550
and will be the US bid

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.550
in a worldwide battle for the 2024 games.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.427
The polling was very clear that at first,

00:08:59.427 --> 00:09:01.860
residents of Greater Boston

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:05.910
supported the Boston 2024 Olympic bid.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.610
The idea of the Olympics

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:12.180
improving the public transportation
system in Massachusetts

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.330
was always the most
appealing and enticing part.

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.850
But what became clear is that over time,

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.260
the more people learned about
the implications of the bid,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:23.970
the risks, and the costs,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.070
that they got beyond those glossy images,

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.540
and saw what it would really mean

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.210
for their lives and for
the future of the state,

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:34.470
that people's support for the
games dropped dramatically.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.030
We see that the bid requires

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:39.810
building the three most
expensive Olympic venues,

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:42.813
that every Olympics since
1960 has gone over budget.

00:09:44.621 --> 00:09:46.186
We felt that there needed

00:09:46.186 --> 00:09:47.340
to be a real debate about
whether the Olympics

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.310
was the right thing for
Massachusetts or not.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:54.090
And we saw that because
of the power of the people

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.561
pushing the bid, there were
gonna be very few institutions,

00:09:57.561 --> 00:10:00.630
that would normally ask
tough questions of the bid,

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.040
that were gonna do that in this case.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.040
And so that opposition had
to come from the grassroots,

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.240
and that's why we formed
No Boston Olympics.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.240
Boston 2024's original proposal

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.630
was to put beach volleyball
here in Boston Common,

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.720
not a place that has sand,
not a place along the water.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.540
And one of the most
historic public spaces,

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.970
and important public spaces in the US.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.470
This is the original public
park in North America.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.180
Boston was the first major city

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.560
to stand up and say no
to the summer 2024 games.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.770
But in the wake of our decision in Boston,

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:42.630
other cities dropped out.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:44.820
Rome, Italy had been
talking about bidding.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.500
A new mayor was elected there who said

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.960
she had more important
priorities than the Olympics,

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.790
and so that bid died.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.800
Hamburg, Germany, actually
had a public referendum

00:10:54.796 --> 00:10:56.580
to bid on the 2024 games,

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:00.090
and voted that down in November of 2015.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.180
Budapest Hungary, there was a grassroots

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.160
opposition to the games there,

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.560
that forced the question of the Olympics

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:08.610
to be on the ballot.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.710
And eventually the
organizers of the games there

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:13.110
decided not to move forward with it.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.150
In 2017, left with a dwindling

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:18.480
set of potential hosts for the 2024 games.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.040
The International Olympic Committee

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.350
made an unprecedented move.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:25.920
It awarded the two remaining
contenders at the same time.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.150
Paris for 2024 and Los Angeles for 2028,

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.430
giving the IOC some extra breathing room

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:35.550
and a chance to focus
on a series of reforms

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.454
You have to listen to critics as well.

00:11:38.454 --> 00:11:40.620
And we've been in contact with them,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.450
and other opponents to the games.

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.160
Because this is our mission,

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:47.673
to listen and to make
sure that their analysis,

00:11:48.746 --> 00:11:50.790
we do understand why they
reached these conclusions,

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.606
and what we can improve.

00:11:52.606 --> 00:11:54.360
In the late 1970s,

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.300
the IOC was in a similarly
desperate position

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.484
facing backlash after
a series of blemished

00:12:00.484 --> 00:12:01.710
events earlier in the decade.

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.020
Sensing a rare opportunity for leverage,

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.330
the city of Los Angeles
decided that it could make

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:10.233
the 1984 Olympics a
success, but on its terms.

00:12:11.399 --> 00:12:14.070
What happened in '84 was
good for Los Angeles,

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:16.830
it turned out for some
very interesting reasons.

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:17.820
But it was a disaster,

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.820
'cause the IOC had to give up
on many of its requirements.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.510
The IOC requires for instance,
that a host city guarantee,

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:28.290
that it will carry out all
of the tasks in its contract,

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:31.560
and take full responsibility financially

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.510
for any costs that are involved.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.400
In 1984, because Los Angeles

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.170
was the only city that was bidding,

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:40.200
the IOC basically was on its knees

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.420
in front of the Los Angeles committee

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.250
saying, "Please, please do this."

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.410
And so the Los Angeles
Committee dictated terms,

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:47.490
and one of the terms was,

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.040
we're not gonna be
responsible for cost overruns.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:50.880
By most accounts,

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:55.080
the 1984 Los Angeles Summer
Games were a resounding success.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.350
Instead of saddling taxpayers
with a catastrophic loss,

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.180
the games were actually profitable,

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.670
thanks to the strategic
corporate sponsorships

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:04.800
and manageable costs.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.930
Because the metropolitan area already

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.570
had suitable stadiums,
venues, and housing,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.640
very little new construction was needed,

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.800
preventing the sort of surprise expenses

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.663
that had played earlier host cities.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.430
And now, amid another challenging
period for the Olympics,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.130
Los Angeles is again trying
to rewrite the narrative.

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.530
We like to say that the
LA '84 games are actually

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.570
what gives us the ability
to have such great

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:30.180
public support here in
Los Angeles for the games.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:31.680
You can't walk around this city

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.570
and not meet somebody who has
a positive memory of 1984.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:39.510
And that's really the
starting place for us.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:40.860
We are a no build games.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.680
And sometimes we like to say
it's a part of our radical

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.440
reuse philosophy around sustainability.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.630
The fact is the best venues

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:50.730
are the ones you don't have to build.

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.620
Right over my shoulder, you can see

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:56.070
one of the main pieces that
will actually be the secret

00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.860
sauce of LA being able to
host the Olympic games,

00:13:58.860 --> 00:13:59.970
and that's UCLA.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:02.910
We have UCLA and USC within really a,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.130
just about 10 or 12 miles of each other.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.860
And really that's the beginning
stages of infrastructure.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.090
But beyond that, you know,

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:12.120
we really have just an
extraordinary number of venues

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:15.360
here in LA and in greater
Southern California,

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.850
whether it's three
facilities, three stadiums,

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.160
that are over 75,000 seats,

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.610
multiple arenas, different
types of facilities.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.350
And so we're just tailor made to be able

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.420
to actually host a mega sporting event,

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.490
like the Olympics and the Paralympics.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.620
As for potential cost overruns,

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:33.483
LA '28 has a plan in mind.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:37.290
We actually are carrying a 10% contingency

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.150
from day one in our budget,

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:42.060
in terms of how and what it is
that we actually think about.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.639
And so we're trying to build
against that contingency.

00:14:45.639 --> 00:14:48.420
I think it's incumbent upon all of us

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.340
to think about everything that we do,

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:53.640
and think about it through
the lens of sustainability.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:57.390
We'll have probably just shy
of a hundred thousand jobs,

00:14:57.390 --> 00:15:01.101
that will be a part of our
journey over the next six years.

00:15:01.101 --> 00:15:02.970
And that's billions of
dollars of economic impact

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:04.991
directly to citizens of LA.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.640
Beyond the raw cost benefit figures,

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:09.570
there is another value

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.970
to the Olympics that is harder to measure.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:14.340
For us, it's really about human legacy.

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:15.270
And it starts actually

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.250
with the kids that we want to be sitting

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:18.840
in the stadiums thinking and dreaming,

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.573
that they too can be an
Olympian or a Paralympian.

00:15:23.122 --> 00:15:24.083
It was a part of our Play LA program,

00:15:25.228 --> 00:15:26.061
which is our $160 million

00:15:26.061 --> 00:15:27.540
investment into kids in Los Angeles.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:31.290
One of the real important parts
of that is we've introduced

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.750
adaptive sports into the
recs and park program

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:34.713
in the city of LA.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:39.360
I'm optimistic about the '28
games for the same reasons.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.890
I think that they have really excellent

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.740
management of the organizing committee,

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:45.180
and they're Los Angeles.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.200
They've got a lot more stadiums

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.940
now than they had back in 1984,

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.170
and they've got more infrastructure,

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.900
including public transportation
that they didn't have.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.250
So I think it'll turn
out, it'll turn out well.

00:15:55.649 --> 00:15:59.460
Meanwhile, at the other
end of the spectrum,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:03.000
there are places where costs
simply aren't a major concern.

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.330
But this path comes with
its own set of problems.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:09.390
The person who really seems
to have wanted to host

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.670
the World Cup more than anyone else

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.220
is Sheik Hamad bin Khalifa Al Thani.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:16.470
He's now known as the Father Emir.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:19.680
He was the former ruler of Qatar.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.390
And this was really his desire,

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:25.920
not just to host, you know, a
massive football tournament.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.420
He knew that it would drive

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:30.570
an enormous amount of
development in the country,

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:34.479
that Qatar really needed to
become a kind of modern place.

00:16:34.479 --> 00:16:36.360
And that's exactly what happened.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:37.950
In the last decade alone,

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.220
Qatar has built more roads,
bicycle paths, and bridges,

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:43.833
than during the entire
previous half century.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:48.480
Funded by a robust petroleum
and natural gas based economy,

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.390
the government was less
concerned with short term profit,

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:52.890
and more interested in the long term

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.480
benefits of future tourism,

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.363
business development, and global prestige.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.090
For instance, they've
gone to great lengths

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.470
and no doubt expense,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.380
to show off cutting
edge engineering feats,

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.420
such as this innovative stadium design

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.790
constructed partly out
of shipping containers,

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:12.570
meant to be easily deconstructed
and transported elsewhere.

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:14.820
But the attention the nation has received

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:17.280
has generally not been so positive.

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.070
Behind the scenes, many
more are being exploited,

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.620
unpaid, forced labor, badly treated.

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:24.960
It's very hard to give an exact figure.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.850
I would suspect it's
actually in the hundreds.

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:28.740
I think hundreds of workers

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.599
have died to make this World Cup possible.

00:17:31.599 --> 00:17:36.599
There was initially reports
about the deaths of workers

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:39.570
in stadium projects and other projects

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.180
directly tied to the World Cup.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.630
This obviously set off an
enormous international outcry,

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:48.810
and ultimately the government did start

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:52.020
making reforms in order
to respond to this.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:54.420
So people were living in very cramped,

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:57.120
unsafe, unsanitary accommodations.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:17:59.010
They've set minimum standards on that.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:02.880
They also introduced a
universal minimum wage,

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:06.300
meaning it applies to
people of all nationalities.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.360
It's not much, it's about $274 a month.

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.200
But it's something,

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:12.840
and in fact, it's the first
universal minimum wage

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:15.283
across the gulf region generally.

00:18:15.283 --> 00:18:17.760
Activists are not entirely happy

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.790
with the reforms that
Qatari government has made.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:23.460
They don't believe that
some of these reforms

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:24.900
have gone far enough.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.420
On worker deaths, for instance,

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:31.380
nobody really had bothered
to count for a long time

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:34.020
how many workplace deaths and injuries

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.510
you had across the country.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:37.920
Activists are very concerned about

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:41.640
what happens after everyone goes home.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.264
I think these are questions
we don't know the answer to.

00:18:44.264 --> 00:18:46.710
Qatar's hope is that in the long run,

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:48.630
memories of compelling matches

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:51.660
will outlast any lingering controversy.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.790
The 2022 World Cup story

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.980
echoes other mega-events in recent years,

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:58.500
where strong centralized governments

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:02.430
have spent lavishly to
help build global prestige,

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:05.430
only to find themselves
marred by controversy.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:06.810
If you look for example

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:10.327
at the 2022 Winter
Olympic bidding process,

00:19:10.327 --> 00:19:15.120
all of the democracies that
were bidding in that process

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.540
actually dropped out before
the final bidding phase.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.210
The only two cities that were left

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:24.030
were Beijing, China
and Almaty, Kazakhstan,

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:26.880
both autocratic countries,

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.070
where there's no democracy,
there's no input,

00:19:29.070 --> 00:19:31.920
there's no opportunity
for residents to push back

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:35.053
against the extravagance of the games.

00:19:35.053 --> 00:19:37.170
Russia received a 4-year ban

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:40.140
for doping from the
world Anti-Doping Agency.

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:41.430
China has been criticized

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.230
by some countries and human rights groups

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.860
over what they say is abuse
against Uyghur Muslims.

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:48.060
They're gonna be under increasing

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.980
pressures from corporate sponsors.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:51.780
They're creating all sorts of problems,

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:54.030
in terms of how the
labor force is treated,

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.730
in terms of environmental
destruction, and so on.

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.580
So corporations are sort of pulling back.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:02.040
Or they're saying, "Well, if
you expect us to stay around,

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:03.662
you've gotta clean this stuff up."

00:20:03.662 --> 00:20:05.820
Mounting public concerns

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.550
from citizens, cities, and sponsors,

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:11.100
over the recent state
of controversial games

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:12.420
have forced organizing bodies,

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:16.050
like FIFA and the IOC, to pursue reforms.

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:19.050
One approach to address
the sustainability concern

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:20.550
has been to spread out mega-events

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:23.040
over a region or even a continent,

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.310
instead of forcing a single
city to take on the burden.

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.530
I think FIFA has an easier job,

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:30.210
only because there are more places

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:33.498
that have the necessary
venues and infrastructure.

00:20:33.498 --> 00:20:37.050
And because of the very
profound, and lasting,

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:39.720
and pervasive popularity of soccer,

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.420
they have more leeway,
I think, than the IOC.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:45.797
So hopefully, we'll see
some good changes there.

00:20:45.797 --> 00:20:47.010
Ends of vote...
In fact, things

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:47.843
are already

00:20:47.843 --> 00:20:49.388
looking differently.
Starts now.

00:20:49.388 --> 00:20:51.077
In the next World Cup,

00:20:51.077 --> 00:20:53.040
which will be spread out in 16
cities across North America,

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:55.593
and rely mostly on
existing infrastructure.

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:01.670
The World Cup is coming
back to Boston in 2026.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.440
And it never crossed our minds

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:08.100
to create a No Boston World Cup group,

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:11.130
because the costs are just
gonna be a small fraction

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:13.323
of what hosting the
Olympics would've been.

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:15.090
As for the Olympics,

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.160
the IOC has spent the past decade

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:19.290
reflecting on its complex legacy,

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.330
and outlining broad new reforms

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:22.980
aimed at reinventing the games

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:26.280
for the sustainability
conscious 21st century.

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:29.040
Listen, the most important
was a decade ago,

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:31.100
when we adopted a motto that,

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:33.540
"A city does not adapt to the games,

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.580
but the games adapt to a city."

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:38.520
Then the second thing
which was important is,

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:41.370
we don't build if it's
not absolutely needed.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:43.110
We have a sustainability agenda,

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.789
that is very appealing as well.

00:21:45.789 --> 00:21:48.780
It'll be climate positive
as a rule from 2030,

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:52.920
but already Paris 2024
will be climate positive,

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.730
which is a great objective to reach.

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:57.810
I think everybody also is recognizing

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:00.030
the efforts when it
comes to sustainability,

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:03.900
and the whole agenda, economic,
social, and environmental.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:06.690
Yes, it's more to deliver
for the organizers,

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:08.040
but for the better.

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:11.610
A successful Olympics is one
that, first and foremost,

00:22:11.610 --> 00:22:15.480
brings back to the community
what they were expecting.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:16.920
Even the bidding process,

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.810
once a fierce competition that often

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:20.820
encourage the sort of wild promises,

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:24.270
that led to financial
pitfalls, has been reshaped.

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:26.190
It is a partnership now.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:28.327
So we have cities that come to us and say,

00:22:28.327 --> 00:22:29.640
"Listen, what does it take

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:33.008
if I want to organize the games in 2040?"

00:22:33.008 --> 00:22:35.127
And the big difference is that

00:22:35.127 --> 00:22:37.740
the IOC is not judging anymore

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.950
the quality of a given value proposition.

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.074
It is contributing to it.

00:22:43.074 --> 00:22:45.240
The plans for upcoming games,

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:50.240
Los Angeles in 2028, Milan in
2030, and Brisbane in 2032,

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:54.120
already seem to be heeding
the warning signs of the past,

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:55.323
so far at least.

00:22:56.190 --> 00:23:00.030
But some critics have proposed
an even more radical concept.

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:01.350
What I would favor in the long run,

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:02.490
what I think is most rational,

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.040
particularly in these
days of climate change,

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.900
and the need to conserve resources

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:08.550
and not waste resources,

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:09.720
is we ought to have one city,

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:11.970
that hosts the games permanently.

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.310
Having everyone agree
on a single host city

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:17.040
might be a challenging diplomatic problem.

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:19.110
Regardless, the institutional reforms

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:21.090
within the World Cup and the Olympics

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.920
demonstrate that mega sporting events

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:26.550
can respond to the demands
of an ever-evolving planet,

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:28.800
and the days of massive cost overruns

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.863
and wasteful infrastructure
may be behind us.

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:36.450
I think there are much more
sensible ways to proceed.

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:39.120
Whether the world decides
to be sensible or not,

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.223
I think it's still an undecided question.

